// Brahma's Net offense triggers, and the Upasaka precepts beside them.
// Extracted from the CBETA P5 XML of T24n1484 with the clause splits,
// gates and readings named in the view's own Methodology note. A plain
// script tag, like data.js, so it works over file://.
window.BRAHMA_DATA = {"rows": [{"rank": 1, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 34", "name": "Guarding the aspiration", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Giving rise to a single thought of the two-vehicle or non-Buddhist mind.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "The bodhisattva aspiration is held moment to moment, and the only offense in the forty-eight located purely in the mind. What is guarded is the aspiration itself changing direction, not the content of any passing thought.", "practical": "The two-vehicle mind means the aspiration itself changing — resolving that your own liberation is the goal. Not doubt, not finding the Pali clearer, not practising samatha.", "daily": "Reviewing the aspiration when you notice it has gone quiet. Not treating a hard day as a breach.", "term": "若起一念二乘外道心者 ('if a single thought of the two-vehicle or non-Buddhist mind arises')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The image of the monks 'tied down by rushes' refers to bhikshus robbed and bound with living grass, who would not free themselves for fear of killing the plants and so remained tied until others released them.", "emend": "", "canon": 13401, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["One grows tired of the great practices one has taken up.", "One runs after another sect.", "One renounces the Great Vehicle and goes after the Small."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "The three conditions are about a movement away, not about a stray thought: weariness with the practices one has undertaken, attraction to another sect, and abandoning the Great Vehicle for the Small. He glosses the clause as 'don't ever have thoughts of only saving yourself and not saving others' — which is the aspiration changing, exactly as the chart reads it. He adds that every single thought is an offense, so the count is by moment, not by episode."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Grades the two halves of the clause differently: a two-vehicle thought is a prescribed offense only, while a non-Buddhist thought is both a natural and a prescribed offense. That gradation does not survive into the English and it matters — it means the śrāvaka half of this precept is the lighter half."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "accords", "text": "Glosses the imagery plainly: vajra means solid and indestructible; the air bladder is a life preserver, and the precepts are what keeps one afloat across the sea of suffering. The six periods are three of the day and three of the night."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 34#1"}, {"rank": 2, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 4", "name": "Lying", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Lying oneself — thereby producing wrong speech, view or action in beings.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Truthfulness is extended past speech into gesture, bearing and the inward act of intending to deceive. The offense completes not in the utterance but in the distortion produced in another's understanding, so the measure is effect rather than technical falsity.", "practical": "Measured by effect on another's understanding, not by technical falsity. Includes true statements arranged to mislead.", "daily": "Overstating what one has read or verified. Letting a bio or credential claim stand more strongly than it should.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 401, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (Chinese)", ["It is a living being.", "One perceives it as a living being.", "One has a deceiving mind.", "One speaks the grave matter — the great false speech: claiming attainment not attained, realisation not realised.", "The person addressed understands."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "The fifth condition is decisive and matches the chart's completing condition: if the hearer does not understand, the karma of false speech is not even formed. It also distinguishes grave from light — only the great false speech of claimed attainment completes the parajika; lesser lies are not grave breaches of this precept."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Sorts false speech into four kinds — outright lying, frivolous speech, double-tongued speech and harsh speech — and into eight forms built from seeing, hearing, sensing and knowing, each said and denied. The bodily and mental channel is glossed as deceiving by gesture, bearing and silence as much as by statement."}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Major 3 大妄語戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Laxer", "bullets": [{"label": "Major false speech — laxer here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Narrower than the Brahma's Net, which makes all deceit the grave offense.", "Here only the great false speech — claiming attainment — is a major precept; ordinary lying, frivolous speech, double-tongued speech and harsh speech fall to the light class.", "The 開緣 are broad: lying to save beings from danger to life, and lying to benefit others where one has no attachment of one's own."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Speech — 不妄語 (no false speech). Of the three karmas, the mouth most easily creates offense: false speech, frivolous speech, harsh speech, double-tongued speech. A disciple who guards his words stills contention before it starts."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "False speech: no offense in derangement; none where a skilful lie saves beings from danger to life; none where one lies to benefit beings with no attachment of one's own. Double-tongued speech: no offense where a divisive word, spoken out of compassion, separates someone from a harmful friend. Harsh speech: no offense where a sharp word wakes someone caught in wrong views."}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 4#1"}, {"rank": 3, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 4", "name": "Lying", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Instructing another to lie — thereby producing wrong speech, view or action in beings.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Truthfulness is extended past speech into gesture, bearing and the inward act of intending to deceive. The offense completes not in the utterance but in the distortion produced in another's understanding, so the measure is effect rather than technical falsity.", "practical": "Procuring a falsehood is the same offense as telling one.", "daily": "Asking someone to cover for you; letting a colleague misrepresent something on your behalf.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 402, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Draws the grave/light line explicitly: 小妄言不屬重犯 (lesser lies are not grave breaches of this precept). Only 說重具 (speaking the grave matter), the great false speech of claiming attainment or realisation one does not have, completes the parajika; ordinary falsehood is caught by the light precepts instead."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Sorts false speech four ways — 妄言 (outright lying), 綺語 (frivolous speech), 兩舌 (double-tongued speech), 惡口 (harsh speech) — and eight ways by sense-channel: saying seen what was unseen and unseen what was seen, and the same for heard, sensed and known. The bodily and mental channel is glossed as 語默威儀 (speech, silence and bearing), deceiving by speech, silence and bearing alike."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 4#2"}, {"rank": 4, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 4", "name": "Lying", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Contriving a lie by indirect means — thereby producing wrong speech, view or action in beings.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Truthfulness is extended past speech into gesture, bearing and the inward act of intending to deceive. The offense completes not in the utterance but in the distortion produced in another's understanding, so the measure is effect rather than technical falsity.", "practical": "Engineering a false impression without stating anything false.", "daily": "Selective disclosure in a translation note or citation; quotes cropped to change the sense.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 403, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 4#3"}, {"rank": 5, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 4", "name": "Lying", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Saying seen what was not seen, or unseen what was seen — thereby producing wrong speech, view or action in beings.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Truthfulness is extended past speech into gesture, bearing and the inward act of intending to deceive. The offense completes not in the utterance but in the distortion produced in another's understanding, so the measure is effect rather than technical falsity.", "practical": "The classical formula. In monastic context its gravest form is a false claim of attainment.", "daily": "Claiming to have consulted a source you have not read. Reporting a meditative experience as more than it was.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 404, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 4#4"}, {"rank": 6, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 4", "name": "Lying", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deceiving by body or by mind — by gesture and bearing, or by the inward intent to deceive — thereby producing wrong speech, view or action in beings.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Truthfulness is extended past speech into gesture, bearing and the inward act of intending to deceive. The offense completes not in the utterance but in the distortion produced in another's understanding, so the measure is effect rather than technical falsity.", "practical": "The text gives 身心妄語 as a single compound, not two separate faults; both channels are named together. Appearance management is in scope, and the offense can form before anything outward is done.", "daily": "Performing serenity or attainment you do not have. Rehearsing an explanation you know will mislead before you deliver it.", "term": "身心妄語 ('lying with body and with mind')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 405, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 4#5"}, {"rank": 7, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 7", "name": "Self-praise and disparagement", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Praising oneself and disparaging another — causing that person to be disparaged as a result.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Competitive positioning is the fault: raising oneself by lowering another. Because concealment of another's merit is named alongside active disparagement, silence becomes actionable, and the offense completes only when the comparison actually costs the other person standing.", "practical": "The comparison is the offense, not the accuracy of either half.", "daily": "Framing your training or credentials against another teacher's. Comparative remarks in a seminar.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 701, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["The person one is speaking to is a living being.", "One knows he is a living being.", "One has the intention to praise oneself and slander others.", "One has the material to praise oneself and slander others.", "The person spoken to understands."]], "fourfold": {"Causes": "Greed for profit, or for reputation and popularity.", "Conditions": "Creating the expedients — finding out a person's shortcomings, discovering things to say against him.", "Methods": "Talking about a person's good and bad points; talking about rights and wrongs.", "Karma": "The other person understands what is being said."}, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "The third condition is a real gate, and it is narrow. The intention meant is wanting to expose the other party's shortcomings SO THAT name and profit come to oneself. If one admonishes a person to help him subdue his faults, that is different. If one speaks of his offenses in order to benefit him, that too is all right. Only where neither motive is present does it become pure slander."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "The fourth condition, 'material', is enumerated: another's social status, honourable or lowly; his occupation, noble or base; his skill or profession, superior or inferior; his offenses, whether he has them or not; his afflictions, light or heavy; his physical characteristics, fine or ugly; his wholesome dharmas, whether he is replete with them or not. Every single phrase of such a conversation constitutes a separate major offense."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "accords", "text": "Confirms the completing condition as the chart has it: boasting of one's own virtue AND concealing the good works of others, THUS causing them to be slandered. Concealment is not incidental — it is half the act."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Disparagement is committed in writing as much as in speech. He notes the paradigm case of two people showing off their good points to each other while exposing the shortcomings of others, for the sake of name and profit."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 7#1"}, {"rank": 8, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 7", "name": "Self-praise and disparagement", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Encouraging others to praise themselves and disparage others — causing that person to be disparaged as a result.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Competitive positioning is the fault: raising oneself by lowering another. Because concealment of another's merit is named alongside active disparagement, silence becomes actionable, and the offense completes only when the comparison actually costs the other person standing.", "practical": "Getting someone else to run the comparison for you.", "daily": "Encouraging a friend to make the case for you in a group chat or committee.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 702, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "A distinction the sutra text does not make: teaching other people to praise ONESELF and slander others is a major offense; teaching another person to praise HIMSELF and slander others is only a light offense. The gravity follows who is being elevated."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 7#2"}, {"rank": 9, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 7", "name": "Self-praise and disparagement", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Advertising one's own virtue — causing another to be disparaged as a result.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Competitive positioning is the fault: raising oneself by lowering another. Because concealment of another's merit is named alongside active disparagement, silence becomes actionable, and the offense completes only when the comparison actually costs the other person standing.", "practical": "Self-advertisement becomes the offense once it costs someone else standing.", "daily": "Posting about one's practice, precepts or study in a register that positions one above peers.", "term": "自揚己德 ('extolling one's own virtue')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 703, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 7#3"}, {"rank": 10, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 7", "name": "Self-praise and disparagement", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Concealing another's good qualities — causing that person to be disparaged as a result.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Competitive positioning is the fault: raising oneself by lowering another. Because concealment of another's merit is named alongside active disparagement, silence becomes actionable, and the offense completes only when the comparison actually costs the other person standing.", "practical": "An omission clause inside a parajika, which is unusual and easy to miss.", "daily": "Letting a misapprehension about a colleague stand. Omitting credit in a paper or a conversation.", "term": "隱他人好事 ('concealing another's good qualities')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 704, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "accords", "text": "'Disparaging others' is glossed as discussing the offenses of other people — so this clause and Major 6 overlap in substance and are distinguished by what the speaker gains. Concealment of another's merit belongs here because it is one half of a comparison the speaker is drawing in his own favour."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 7#4"}, {"rank": 11, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 6", "name": "Speaking faults of the four assemblies", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Speaking the offenses of renunciant or householder bodhisattvas, bhiksus or bhiksunis — thereby broadcasting faults within the Buddhadharma.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "The boundary between inside and outside the community is treated as load-bearing for the Sangha Jewel itself. Faults are to be handled internally, where they can be remedied, and never aired to an audience that can only spectate — which is why a separate precept positively requires raising them within.", "practical": "Your vinaya master's reading requires intent to deprive them of support. Internal admonition is *required* by Minor 5, so the two only cohere if this one targets outward defamation.", "daily": "Forum threads about monastery scandals. Group chats trading stories about a teacher. Anything published about a community's failings.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 601, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["The person one is talking to is a living being.", "One knows he is a living being.", "One has the intent to speak of the offenses.", "The offenses are actually spoken of.", "The person one is speaking to has not received the precepts.", "The person spoken to understands what is being said."]], "fourfold": {"Causes": "Having the intent to speak of the offenses of others.", "Conditions": "The manner of speaking. Flowery speech that sounds well but is still the discussion of another's faults.", "Methods": "What is spoken about — heavy or light offenses, told to those outside the assembly.", "Karma": "Having actually spoken. The karma is created at the moment the hearer understands."}, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "The decisive condition is the AUDIENCE, not the motive: 'discussing' here means speaking to people who have not taken the Bodhisattva Precepts. Spoken to someone who has already taken them, he says, it might not be so heavy an offense. The reasoning is that the violation of any precept is not something that needs to be known to anyone who has not received it."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "accords", "text": "This precept pertains to maintaining the Saṃgha Jewel, and covers the whole sevenfold assembly of the Great Vehicle — bhikshus, bhikshunis, shramaneras, shramanerikas, shikshamanas, upasakas, upasikas — not only the twofold assembly of bhikshus and bhikshunis. A left-home person who speaks of bhikshu or bhikshuni offenses to non-precept-holders violates the Bhikshu or Bhikshuni precepts as well as this one."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "It is both a precept of the nature and a precept of restraint: even civil law requires one who slanders another to make a public apology, so the act is wrong independently of the Buddha's prohibition. That places it alongside killing and stealing rather than among the merely prescribed rules."}, {"src": "Your vinaya master", "stance": "diverges", "text": "Read as requiring INTENT TO DEPRIVE the person of support. On that reading an audience of precept-holders would not matter and a malicious report to another monastic would still be the offense. Hui Seng's reading makes the hearer's status decisive and the motive secondary; the vinaya master's makes the motive decisive and the hearer secondary. They converge in the ordinary case — publicising faults to outsiders — and part company on the edge cases."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "On why the two-vehicle exponents are called 'evil' in the sutra: they are attached to their own standards and limitations, doubt the capacity of the Great Vehicle, and consider themselves alone to be correct — so the sutra admonishes them by that name rather than describing them. He also holds the precept to bar speaking of those who have already lost their precepts through a major offense."}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Major 5 說四眾過戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Laxer", "bullets": [{"label": "Speaking of offenses of the fourfold assembly — laxer here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Names the fourfold assembly where the Brahma's Net commentary extends to the whole sevenfold assembly.", "Otherwise identical in shape, and a major precept in both."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 6#1"}, {"rank": 12, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 6", "name": "Speaking faults of the four assemblies", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Instructing others to speak those offenses — thereby broadcasting faults within the Buddhadharma.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "The boundary between inside and outside the community is treated as load-bearing for the Sangha Jewel itself. Faults are to be handled internally, where they can be remedied, and never aired to an audience that can only spectate — which is why a separate precept positively requires raising them within.", "practical": "Sourcing or amplifying the account is the same act as giving it.", "daily": "Forwarding, quote-posting, feeding a journalist. Amplification counts as authorship here.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 602, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 6#2"}, {"rank": 13, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 43", "name": "Deliberate breach", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately bringing forth the intention to violate the precepts of the āryas, and then deliberately violating any of the orthodox precepts.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Integrity between precept status and conduct, with the offense located at the deciding rather than the doing. Deliberateness is named at both ends — in forming the intention and in the act that follows.", "practical": "Deliberateness now appears at both ends — in forming the intention and in the act. Lapse and failure under pressure fall outside on either reading, but Dharmamitra's text makes it explicit. The clauses about not deserving a dānapati's offerings, not walking on the king's land, and the five thousand ghosts describe the state of a precept-breaker, not further offenses.", "daily": "Deciding in advance that a rule will not apply on a given trip or occasion. And not accepting support offered on the strength of a standing one is not currently holding.", "term": "故起心毀犯聖戒者 ('deliberately raising the intention to violate the holy precepts')", "note": "", "emend": "Restores 'deliberately' to the concluding clause, present in the Fazang, Zhuhong and Zhixu commentaries.", "canon": 14301, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["It is a violation, and one knows that it is a violation.", "One has the intention to commit a violation.", "One decides to transgress."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "The offense is complete at the deciding. 'It prohibits the intent to violate Precepts before one has actually violated them.' He states there are no exceptions to this rule, and that every thought brought forth to deliberately transgress is a separate defiling offense."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "diverges", "text": "Holds this to be a violation against the nature as well as of restraint, and says it does not matter whether the person has received the precepts or not — if in his mind he is planning to violate them, he is already creating an offense. That is wider than the chart's reading, which treats the clause as binding those who have received."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Gives the same three conditions and adds that this precept is 唯遮不開 (prescribed and closed to exemption) — closed to exemption. No circumstance opens it."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 43#1"}, {"rank": 14, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 24", "name": "Abandoning the Great Vehicle for lesser study", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Failing to study and practise the Mahayana diligently and abandoning it — studying wrong-view two-vehicle texts, secular classics, Abhidharma or miscellaneous treatises in its place.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Displacement rather than breadth of reading: the fault is what Mahayana study was traded away for. The two halves are bound by a single correlative, so abandoning and substituting must occur together for the clause to bite.", "practical": "One clause bound by 反 ('instead'). If the Mahayana is the stronghold and other reading is instrumental, the condition is not met.", "daily": "Study hours going to secondary literature and methodology rather than to the sutras themselves. Reading about texts instead of reading texts.", "term": "反學 ('studying instead')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 12401, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (Chinese)", ["One possesses the Mahayana scriptures, right view, right nature and the Dharma-body.", "One fails to study and practise them diligently.", "One studies the two-vehicle, non-Buddhist, secular, Abhidharma or miscellaneous material in their place."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "STATES THE EXCEPTION EXPLICITLY. 'There are exceptions to this Precept. If one needs to understand the doctrines of the Two Vehicles in order to teach and transform those who adhere to their theories, or if one needs to understand the doctrines of externalists in order to take them across, or if one needs to understand worldly doctrines in order to rescue people of the world, then one can study those principles.' The limit follows: 'However, if one studies them with the sole purpose of benefitting oneself and fails to seek Unsurpassed Proper and Equal Right Enlightenment, then it is not permissible.' So the gate is the purpose of the study, not its subject matter."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Grades the breach: beginning to study the two vehicles from the moment one leaves home, cultivating nothing else, is a violation of restraint. Never studying any Buddhadharma at all and only non-Buddhist doctrine is a violation of both the nature and of restraint."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 24#1"}, {"rank": 15, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 33", "name": "Idle spectacle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Listening to instrumental music — conch, drum, horn, zither, flute, harp.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Restraint of the mind's restless casting-about, which the traditional title names directly as initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. The five sub-restrictions are alike in fixing attention on what neither benefits the viewer nor anyone else.", "practical": "Ungated by malice, unlike the watching-fights clause. About habitual consumption, not music as sin.", "daily": "Streaming, playlists, background music while working. The most constant of the whole set.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13301, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["One encounters such a circumstance.", "One has the intent to grasp at the appearance — that is, one wants to get involved.", "One gets hold of an expedient means.", "One actually becomes engaged in the affair."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "EXCEPTION FOR MUSIC: 'This Precept prohibits making music solely for entertainment purposes, but making music as an offering to the Triple Jewel is a different matter; that is permissible.' Liturgical chant and music offered to the Three Jewels fall outside the clause."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Divides the precept into five sub-restrictions — watching fighting or quarrelling, listening to entertaining music, gambling games, divination, and acting as an accomplice to thieves — and defines the evil intent as not acting to benefit people according to their propensities, but from deviant thought."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Grades the five: quarrelling is both a natural and a prescribed offense; the other four are prescribed only, and are graded lightly according to the matter. The five monastic assemblies incur all five. Householders making music as an offering to the Three Jewels do not offend."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 33#1"}, {"rank": 16, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 33", "name": "Idle spectacle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Listening to singing and theatrical performance.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Restraint of the mind's restless casting-about, which the traditional title names directly as initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. The five sub-restrictions are alike in fixing attention on what neither benefits the viewer nor anyone else.", "practical": "Narrative entertainment as a category, not a specific medium.", "daily": "Film, television, series. The bulk of what a phone or laptop is used for in the evening.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13302, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Grades the five sub-restrictions, which the English does not. 鬥諍 (quarrels and fights) — watching quarrels and fights — is 性遮二業 (both a natural and a prescribed offense), both natural and prescribed. The other four are 唯遮罪 (prescribed offenses only)，隨事結輕 (graded lightly according to the matter), prescribed offenses only, graded lightly according to the matter."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Splits the music exemption by status: 出家五眾全犯 (the five monastic assemblies incur them all) — the five monastic assemblies incur all five restrictions; 在家供養三寶，得作妓樂 (householders making music as an offering to the Three Jewels may do so). The English gives the exemption without the lay/monastic distinction."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 33#2"}, {"rank": 17, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 33", "name": "Idle spectacle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Dice and games of chance.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Restraint of the mind's restless casting-about, which the traditional title names directly as initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. The five sub-restrictions are alike in fixing attention on what neither benefits the viewer nor anyone else.", "practical": "Wagering and chance-driven play.", "daily": "Gambling apps, loot mechanics, anything with a randomised reward loop — which includes most infinite feeds.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13303, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 33#3"}, {"rank": 18, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 33", "name": "Idle spectacle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Board games — go, prasena, chess, backgammon, checkers.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Restraint of the mind's restless casting-about, which the traditional title names directly as initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. The five sub-restrictions are alike in fixing attention on what neither benefits the viewer nor anyone else.", "practical": "Absorbing strategic play, named explicitly rather than left to inference.", "daily": "Chess apps, go servers, puzzle games, daily word games. The 'harmless' category the text names directly.", "term": "圍碁 ('go')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13304, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 33#4"}, {"rank": 19, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 33", "name": "Idle spectacle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Ball games, billiards and darts.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Restraint of the mind's restless casting-about, which the traditional title names directly as initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. The five sub-restrictions are alike in fixing attention on what neither benefits the viewer nor anyone else.", "practical": "Sport as absorption, whether played or watched.", "daily": "Watching sport; highlight feeds and coverage.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13305, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 33#5"}, {"rank": 20, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 33", "name": "Idle spectacle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Fortune telling or divination using the fingernail mirror, yarrow stalks, willow branches, bowls or skulls.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Restraint of the mind's restless casting-about, which the traditional title names directly as initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. The five sub-restrictions are alike in fixing attention on what neither benefits the viewer nor anyone else.", "practical": "Consulting oracles for guidance on one's own affairs.", "daily": "Astrology apps, tarot, I Ching for personal decisions. Marginal for many, but the same clause.", "term": "髑髏 ('skull')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The 'fingernail mirror' is a technique in which an elixir is smeared on the fingers and a mantra recited so the palm radiates light like a mirror in which images appear. Prasena is an ancient Indian military board game.", "emend": "", "canon": 13306, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 33#6"}, {"rank": 21, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Minor 8", "name": "Turning from the Great Vehicle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Turning the heart from the Mahayana and declaring in speech that it is not the Buddha's word — taking up wrong-view scriptures and codes in its place.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Public repudiation, not private doubt. Three conditions must join: the heart turning away, the declaration in speech that the teaching is not Buddha-word, and the adoption of a wrong-view code in its place.", "practical": "Three joined conditions. Living under a Vinaya one regards as Buddha-word, while holding the Mahayana as Buddha-word, does not meet the trigger.", "daily": "Academic writing. Stating the historical dating is not this; arguing in print that the Mahayana is therefore not Buddhavacana approaches it.", "term": "心背大乘…言非佛說 ('the heart turns from the Great Vehicle… declaring it not the Buddha's word')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 10801, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 8#1"}, {"rank": 22, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 9", "name": "Anger and refusal of apology", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Reviling with foul speech, or striking with hand, knife or staff — the mind still unquieted, and refusing to relent when the other apologises.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Reconciliation is made mandatory rather than optional. The escalation from foul speech to blows is the setting; the offense completes at the refusal to relent once the other has apologised, so anger that resolves on apology falls outside it entirely.", "practical": "The hinge is the refusal. Anger that resolves on apology is not this offense.", "daily": "Holding a grudge after someone has apologised. Far more common than the striking.", "term": "猶瞋不解者 ('still will not let the anger go')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Fazang's title is shorter than Zhiyi's, which specifies the refusal of apologies. The offense extends to sentient and insentient beings alike.", "emend": "Restores 'kindness' and 'a mind of filial respect' to the positive duty, from the early commentaries and the Song, Yuan and Ming editions.", "canon": 901, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 9#1"}, {"rank": 23, "tier": 1, "tier_label": "Continuous", "precept": "Major 10", "name": "Slandering the Three Jewels", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Slandering the Three Jewels oneself, instructing others to, or abetting evil or wrong-view people in their slander.", "gate": "No faith or reverence arising", "principle": "Faith is held at the level of acute sensitivity — a single word of slander is said to feel like three hundred spears. Because abetting is treated as equivalent to authorship, amplifying another's attack incurs the same weight as making one's own.", "practical": "Gated on the absence of faith. Critical scholarship undertaken from within faith is not the target.", "daily": "Piling on in online arguments about Buddhism. Amplifying a hostile framing for the sake of the argument.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 1001, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 10#1"}, {"rank": 24, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 3", "name": "Eating meat", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately eating any meat whatsoever.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Meat-eating is said to sever the seed-nature of great compassion and to make beings flee at one's approach. The prohibition admits no allowance for food that was merely offered, which is precisely where it parts company with the alms traditions.", "practical": "Absolute in the Chinese tradition, with no allowance for offered food — the divergence from Theravada alms practice.", "daily": "Every meal. On alms round this is the standing friction with what is placed in the bowl.", "term": "斷大慈悲性種子 ('severs the seed-nature of great compassion')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 10301, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 3#1"}, {"rank": 25, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 4", "name": "The five pungent roots", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately eating any of the five pungent plants — garlic, green onions, scallions, alpine leeks, asafoetida — as an ingredient in any food.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Dietary restraint extended from dishes to ingredients, so that the pungent roots are caught wherever they occur. The stated concern is the effect on the body and on those who must sit near one.", "practical": "Scope is unusually explicit: 'in any food' catches these as components, not just as dishes.", "daily": "Reading labels; declining most restaurant and lay-cooked food. High friction, low doctrinal weight.", "term": "一切食中不得食 ('not to be eaten in any food')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 10401, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 4#1"}, {"rank": 26, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Major 1", "name": "Killing", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Taking life by one's own hand — with an unrestrained mind, gleeful in intent.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Culpability is spread across the whole causal chain — intending, contriving, commanding, performing — rather than resting on the final blow. The scope is anything possessing life, so the precept governs ordinary carelessness as much as deliberate violence, and the concluding clause locates the gravity in the quality of mind that takes satisfaction in the act.", "practical": "Rejoicing at a killing is listed among the modes but is not what the concluding clause names as parajika.", "daily": "Insects. Mosquitoes, ants indoors, driving. The daily form for most precept-holders.", "term": "恣心快意 ('unrestrained mind, gleeful in intent')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: He frames the whole mode-list inside a single offense clause, so that praising a killing and according with and delighting in seeing others kill both read as offense modes. But he opens all ten major precepts with the same bracketed 'it is an offense if' construction, so this is a translational convention across the set rather than evidence about rejoicing in particular — it does not show that the concluding clause is not doing the work.", "emend": "Restores the 'on the contrary' phrase from the Zhiyi and Zhuhong commentaries and the Song and Yuan editions — the same pivot that separates the ideal from the charge throughout.", "canon": 101, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 10 飲蟲水戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Drinking water that contains bugs — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Grouped under killing.", "The Brahma's Net has nothing this fine-grained; here the standard reaches to what one drinks without looking."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 27 養蠶戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Raising silkworms — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Grouped under killing.", "A specific livelihood the Brahma's Net does not name."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Killing and harming life — 不殺傷眾生 (not killing or wounding beings). 我等與法界眾生本是同一體性 (we and all beings of the Dharma realm are of one substance); every being has the buddha nature and is fit to become a buddha. Life is equal and alike. One must not merely refrain from harming, but actively protect and nourish life."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "No offense where death is caused without any intent to kill. No offense in severe derangement, defined as seizing fire as though it were gold and filth as though it were sandalwood; anything short of that is not derangement, and the offense is grave."}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 1#1"}, {"rank": 27, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Major 1", "name": "Killing", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Instructing another to take life — with an unrestrained mind, gleeful in intent.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Culpability is spread across the whole causal chain — intending, contriving, commanding, performing — rather than resting on the final blow. The scope is anything possessing life, so the precept governs ordinary carelessness as much as deliberate violence, and the concluding clause locates the gravity in the quality of mind that takes satisfaction in the act.", "practical": "Extends to delegation and request, not only to command.", "daily": "Asking for pest control; requesting a dish be prepared; delegating an animal's disposal.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 102, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 1#2"}, {"rank": 28, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Major 1", "name": "Killing", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Adopting expedient means to kill, praising an act of killing, or killing by mantra — with an unrestrained mind, gleeful in intent.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Culpability is spread across the whole causal chain — intending, contriving, commanding, performing — rather than resting on the final blow. The scope is anything possessing life, so the precept governs ordinary carelessness as much as deliberate violence, and the concluding clause locates the gravity in the quality of mind that takes satisfaction in the act.", "practical": "The praising half makes approval of a killing already done an independent act.", "daily": "Setting traps or poison. Approving a culling, a war action or an execution in conversation.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 103, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 1#3"}, {"rank": 29, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Major 2", "name": "Stealing", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Stealing by one's own hand — deliberately, anything owned, down to a needle or a blade of grass.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Property is protected downward to the trivial and outward to the disreputable owner, so that no taking is too small to count and no owner too undeserving to be robbed. The precept treats the boundary of what is given as absolute, which is what makes it a daily discipline rather than an occasional one.", "practical": "The scope is what makes this daily rather than rare: taking anything not given.", "daily": "Office supplies, software licences, paywalled material, unattributed text. In community, using shared property outside its designation.", "term": "一針一草 ('a needle, a blade of grass')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 201, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Stealing — 不偷盜 (no stealing). Taking what is not given is theft. It stains the naturally pure self-nature, harms beings, and brings loss upon them."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "From the Yogacara precept text: a bodhisattva who sees robbers seizing goods, saṅgha property or stupa property may take them back out of compassion — but must return every item to its owner. Keeping any fraction makes him the thief. Derangement, as under killing."}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 2#1"}, {"rank": 30, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Major 2", "name": "Stealing", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Encouraging another to steal, adopting expedient means to steal, or stealing by mantra — deliberately, anything owned.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Property is protected downward to the trivial and outward to the disreputable owner, so that no taking is too small to count and no owner too undeserving to be robbed. The precept treats the boundary of what is given as absolute, which is what makes it a daily discipline rather than an occasional one.", "practical": "Covers indirect acquisition through a willing intermediary.", "daily": "Asking someone to share credentials or pass on material they are not entitled to give.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 202, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 2#2"}, {"rank": 31, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 44", "name": "Honouring the scriptures", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "General orientation", "literal": "Failing to honour the Mahayana texts in accordance with the Dharma — illustrated by upholding and reciting them single-mindedly, copying out the Buddha's precepts, and keeping them in containers with incense and flowers. (Flaying skin for paper and pricking blood for ink is hyperbolic register; bark, paper, silk, cloth and bamboo are explicitly permitted in the next breath.)", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Texts are treated as objects of offering rather than merely of information, and the offense is located in the absence of a reverent mind. The five named ways of making offering are what the duty consists in.", "practical": "An orientation precept. The offense is a general failure of reverence toward the texts, not the omission of any one listed act.", "daily": "Actual recitation rather than only reading. Transcription and digitisation count as copying. Not stacking things on sutras.", "term": "若不如法供養者 ('if he does not make offerings in accordance with the Dharma')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The permitted writing materials are tree bark, mulberry paper, white silk, bamboo slats and silk cloth — supplied immediately after the skin-and-blood imagery, so no literal reading is required.", "emend": "", "canon": 14401, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["Receive and uphold the texts.", "Read and recite them.", "Write them out — writing out the sutras is itself an act of offering.", "Explain them for others.", "Adorn them with flowers, incense and jewels."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "These five are the ways of making offering, so failing any of them is where the offense lies — which makes the clause considerably more tractable than 'honour the texts'. He classes it as a precept of restraint only: the one who lacks reverence for the Dharma commits it."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "accords", "text": "Also a prescribed offense only, incurred 因無敬法心 (because of the absence of a mind that reveres the Dharma). Confirms that the offense is located in the attitude, not in any particular act of adornment."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 44#1"}, {"rank": 32, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 1", "name": "Respect to teachers and elders", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "General orientation", "literal": "Failing in reverence toward a senior member of the Saṃgha, an upādhyāya, an ācārya, a greatly virtuous fellow student, or one who holds the same views or cultivates the same practices — illustrated by not rising to welcome him, not bowing in reverence and then half-bowing with joined palms, and not making offerings in accordance with the Dharma. (The offer of one's body, cities, children and treasures is hyperbolic register.)", "gate": "Arrogance / pride / delusion / hatred", "principle": "Deference is expressed bodily and immediately, in the moment of encounter, against the pride that would withhold it. The gate names the disqualifying states, so the fault lies in the arrogance that prevents the gesture rather than in any lapse of attention.", "practical": "An orientation precept, gated on the mind — a genuine failure to notice is not the offense.", "daily": "Standing when a senior enters. Constant in any hall with an established order.", "term": "若不爾者 ('if not so')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Fazang's title names only seniors as the objects of the disrespect; Zhiyi's names both seniors and peers, which is the reading followed here. 'Senior member of the Saṃgha' means a bhikshu of at least thirty years since higher ordination.", "emend": "Restores 'a hateful mind' to the list of disqualifying states, on the strength of its presence in the Zhiyi commentary and the Song, Yuan and Ming editions.", "canon": 10101, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 1 不供養父母師長戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Failure to make offerings to parents, teachers and elders — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["The Brahma's Net names teachers and seniors; this adds parents, and makes the omission itself the offense.", "Its group is 惟遮不開 (prescribed, and closed to exemption)."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Offerings to parents, teachers and the Three Jewels — 孝親尊師敬三寶 (be filial to kin, honour teachers, revere the Three Jewels). Parents and teachers are the field of kindness; the Three Jewels are the unsurpassed field of merit. Both are to be revered and supported."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "惟遮不開 (prescribed, and closed to exemption)."}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 1#1"}, {"rank": 33, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 38", "name": "Sitting in order", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Not sitting in order of precept-reception — whoever received earlier ahead, later behind, overriding age, monastic gender, nobility, royalty and servile status.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Order of reception displaces age, rank, birth and wealth, and the list of things it overrides is deliberately provocative. The principle is stated positively: in this Dharma, the earlier sits first and the later sits after.", "practical": "Reader's reading: it does not specify which precepts, so it applies to whatever reception governs the hall — higher ordination where that is the operative order.", "daily": "Not accepting a forward seat offered for one's education, languages or usefulness. Small refusals, constantly.", "term": "先受戒者在前坐 ('whoever received first sits in front')", "note": "", "emend": "Restores 'does not accord with the Dharma in each and every case' to the concluding clause, preserved in nearly all the early commentaries but lost from every edition but the Ming.", "canon": 13801, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 25 在五眾前行戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Walking ahead of the fivefold assembly — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Extends the seniority principle from where one sits to where one walks."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 38#1"}, {"rank": 34, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 5", "name": "Not prompting repentance", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Failing to instruct a transgressor to repent and failing to raise the charge — while continuing to live with them, share the community's support, and hold poshadha and precept-recitation alongside them.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Shared communal life carries an obligation that silence cannot discharge. The offense is cumulative — knowing, saying nothing, and continuing to share support and recitation with the person — which is what distinguishes it from the grave precept against airing faults outside.", "practical": "Cumulative: knowing, saying nothing, and going on sharing communal life together complete it. This is what makes Major 6 an outward-facing rule rather than a gag.", "daily": "The default of not mentioning things. Applies wherever precepts are shared and recited together.", "term": "舉其罪 ('raise his offense')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The seven heinous offenses are drawing the blood of a buddha, killing one's father, killing one's mother, killing one's upādhyāya, killing one's ācārya, breaking up a Dharma-wheel-turning Saṃgha proceeding, and killing an ārya.", "emend": "", "canon": 10501, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 6 見四眾毀戒心生憍慢戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Arrogance on seeing the fourfold assembly violate precepts — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Where the Brahma's Net requires raising a transgressor's offense, this adds that one may not feel superior on witnessing it.", "The disposition is policed, not only the omission."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Reverence toward the Three Jewels — 恭敬三寶 (revere the Three Jewels). The Dharma is obtained through reverence. Revering the Three Jewels breaks down arrogance and self-importance."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "No offense through sloth, inattention or forgetfulness — that is an undefiled breach. No offense in grave illness or disorder of mind. No offense in not bowing while a senior is speaking Dharma or a serious matter is being deliberated, nor while one is oneself speaking or hearing Dharma. No offense where one refrains to protect the speaker's mind, to bring someone to discipline, 若護僧制 (to uphold the saṅgha)s regulations' to uphold the saṅgha's own regulations, or to protect the feelings of the many."}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 5#1"}, {"rank": 35, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 22", "name": "Pride preventing instruction", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Relying on one's own intelligence, upper-class origins, seniority in age, family name, aristocratic background, understanding, merit or wealth, and out of that arrogance and pride refusing to seek out and receive teachings on the sutras and moral codes from the first teacher.", "gate": "Arrogance", "principle": "A teacher's merit is independent of birth, age, wealth and physical condition. The offense is the refusal of instruction, and pride is named as its cause — so the fault is not the judgement but what the judgement prevents.", "practical": "The failure is refusing the instruction; pride is the named cause.", "daily": "Discounting a teacher who lacks one's own formal or academic training. A live risk for anyone academically trained among practitioners who are not.", "term": "", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The teacher who is disdained may be of lesser family, younger, of inferior social station, poor, or physically disabled, and yet exhaustively versed in the sutras and moral codes.", "emend": "Restores 'great wealth' to the list of advantages the arrogant student presumes upon.", "canon": 12201, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 22#1"}, {"rank": 36, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 2", "name": "Alcohol", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately consuming intoxicants, or instructing or causing any person or being to consume them.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Intoxicants are the causal condition out of which other offenses arise, which is why the prohibition is absolute rather than proportionate. Procurement and encouragement are named alongside consumption.", "practical": "Absolute, and lighter than the major precept, which concerns selling.", "daily": "On this reading the precept reaches well past alcohol: cannabis, and any habitual psychoactive use outside a therapeutic setting.", "term": "", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: He renders the term as 'intoxicants' rather than 'alcoholic beverages' by deliberate choice, holding that the precept covers any recreationally or habitually consumed substance that beclouds, dulls, deranges or addicts the mind or body — he names cannabis and THC, methedrine, opiates, MDMA, DMT, and psychedelics taken in a non-therapeutic setting. This is his interpretive judgement, stated as such.", "emend": "", "canon": 10201, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 2 耽樂飲酒戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Indulging in intoxicants for pleasure — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Covers alcohol, anaesthetics, tobacco, drugs and 任何能使人喪失理智之藥品 (anything that costs a person his reason).", "The reasoning is social as much as personal: 一旦上癮更是害人害己；破壞家庭及社會 (once addicted one harms others and oneself, and wrecks family and society)."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 2#1"}, {"rank": 37, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 45", "name": "Teaching beings", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "General orientation", "literal": "Failing to teach and transform beings — illustrated by calling out to people in towns and houses that they should take the three refuges and ten precepts, and by addressing any animal seen so that it raises the aspiration.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Closes off the solitary practitioner who withdraws and stops attending to beings. The clause is triggered by an opportunity encountered and declined, not by a duty to manufacture occasions.", "practical": "The offense clause is the general failure to teach and transform, not the omission of any specific utterance. The calling-out and the address to animals are hyperbolic illustration of the orientation.", "daily": "Whether beings are within one's field of concern at all, or whether practice has become private. Willingness to speak when there is an opening.", "term": "若不教化眾生者 ('if he does not teach and transform beings')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: His concluding clause is 'if this bodhisattva fails to teach and transform beings' — the calling out to people and the address to animals precede it as illustration, confirming that the trigger is the general failure rather than any specific utterance.", "emend": "", "canon": 14501, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["It is a living creature.", "One knows that it is a living creature.", "One does not have a mind to teach and transform it.", "One actually fails to teach it."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "accords", "text": "'When one encounters an opportunity to teach and fails to teach, that's an offense.' The offense is triggered by an opportunity encountered, not by a duty to manufacture one — which is what the chart's orientation reading holds. A precept of restraint."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Ties it to the first of the four great vows taken at refuge — 'Living beings are boundless; I vow to save them all.' Failing to teach and transform beings is violating that vow, which is why the clause is framed as an orientation rather than a script."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 45#1"}, {"rank": 38, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 10", "name": "Storing weapons", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately collecting any knives or clubs.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "One who does not retaliate even for the killing of his parents has no use for arms, so the fault lies in possession rather than in use. The concluding clause narrows to blades and staves what the preceding prohibition lists far more widely.", "practical": "Possession is the offense, not use. The narrowing stands on both texts: the concluding clause names only knives and clubs, while the wider list — bows, arrows, spears, battle-axes, nets and other killing equipment — sits in the preceding prohibition with no penalty of its own.", "daily": "A knife kept for defence rather than for work. Relevant to what is carried while travelling.", "term": "", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Zhiyi is not followed here on a related point: Dharmamitra declines his suggested emendation about burning, judging the reasoning unconvincing.", "emend": "Substitutes 'kill' for 'other' in the preceding clause, giving 'how much the less would he kill any living being' rather than 'how much the less any other being'.", "canon": 11001, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 10#1"}, {"rank": 39, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 32", "name": "Harming beings", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately collecting knives, clubs, or bows and arrows; or raising cats, raccoon dogs, pigs or dogs.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Instruments and arrangements that structurally enable harm or exploitation — arms, false measures, coercive seizure, and the binding or wrecking of what others have built.", "practical": "Read in its series this concerns animals kept for use or slaughter rather than companionship, though the text does not say so.", "daily": "Monastery animals, which many communities keep.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13201, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 16 蓄養畜獸不淨施 (pure giving)戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Raising animals without performing pure giving — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Adds a requirement the Brahma's Net does not have: an animal kept must be formally given away (淨施 (pure giving)) rather than owned outright."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 32#1"}, {"rank": 40, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 27", "name": "Accepting a separate invitation", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Accepting an individually directed invitation — taking its gains into one's own possession and using them for oneself.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Offerings directed to the universal Sangha may not be diverted to private use. The offense completes at using them for oneself rather than at receiving them, and the whole cluster presupposes a resident community with a roster and an officer to administer it.", "practical": "Completes at 'using it for oneself', not at receiving. The cluster presupposes a resident community with a roster and an officer — alms round and solitary retreat are not in its field of vision.", "daily": "In traditions where individually designated offerings are ordinary, this diverges from local norm. The alternative to declining is redirecting to the community.", "term": "自己用故 ('because of using it for oneself')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The eight fields of merit are the Buddha, the āryas, the upādhyāyas, the ācāryas, members of the Saṃgha, one's father, one's mother, and the sick. Dharmamitra's rendering keeps 'for himself alone' in the prohibition and 'diverts to one's own use' in the concluding clause, so the appropriation reading stands.", "emend": "", "canon": 12701, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 27#1"}, {"rank": 41, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Major 3", "name": "Sexual misconduct", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Sexual activity oneself, with any woman, with animals or non-human beings, or by improper means — without a compassionate mind.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Sexual conduct is treated as the root of continued birth and death, so the precept is total for a monastic and narrowed to misconduct for a householder. The completing clause locates the fault in the absence of compassion, making the state of mind part of the act rather than a mitigating circumstance.", "practical": "For laity the corresponding scope is misconduct, not celibacy — which is what makes Minor 36's oath awkward to recite as a layman.", "daily": "As a monastic, absolute. As a layman, the ordinary scope.", "term": "非道行婬 ('intercourse by improper means')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: 'Contrary to the path' is read by the commentators as oral or anal relations, relations with mother, daughter or sister, or relations at wrong times — for a married layman, for instance, while a wife is pregnant or nursing.", "emend": "", "canon": 301, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (English)", ["The situation — there is a possibility of engaging in sexual conduct.", "Thought — one has the thought of sexual desire.", "The actual event transpires."]], "fourfold": {"Causes": "A defiled mind.", "Conditions": "To look at, and then run after, the object.", "Methods": "The means which facilitate pursuing the object.", "Karma": "The actual sexual conduct transpires."}, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Places the precept third deliberately: it is not as heavy as killing or stealing, but for one seeking to leave the triple realm it obstructs more than either. He glosses the act as 'conduct which is not Brahma-like' — Brahma meaning pure — and as the root of birth and death."}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 20 非時非處行欲戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Conjugal relations at the wrong times or places — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["The Brahma's Net leaves the lay scope at 邪婬 (sexual misconduct) without specifying.", "This adds time and place restrictions for married couples — 若非時非處行欲，則傷身敗性，違背禮儀 (relations at the wrong time or place harm the body, mar the nature, and go against propriety)."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Sexual misconduct — 不邪婬 (sexual misconduct) bù xiéyín, 'no sexual misconduct'. Lust is the root of birth and death, binding beings so they cannot escape."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "No offense in deep sleep, in derangement, or where one is seized by an enemy and the experience is 惟苦無樂 (suffering only, no pleasure) — suffering with no pleasure in it."}, {"label": "Standards of conduct — not offense clauses, but what the lay community holds", "items": ["Do not look at anything that goes against propriety — 'our eyes are a medium of lust, so too is our smile.'", "Do not read indecent books, or watch indecent film or video.", "Do not draw near to or associate with those in the habit of lustful behaviour.", "Do not wear revealing clothing."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 3#1"}, {"rank": 42, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Major 3", "name": "Sexual misconduct", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Encouraging others to engage in sexual relations, or leading everyone into them — not restricting oneself even from animals, or from one's mother, daughters, sisters or other close relatives — behaving utterly devoid of compassion.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Sexual conduct is treated as the root of continued birth and death, so the precept is total for a monastic and narrowed to misconduct for a householder. The completing clause locates the fault in the absence of compassion, making the state of mind part of the act rather than a mitigating circumstance.", "practical": "Concerns effect rather than only intent.", "daily": "How one presents and conducts oneself; awareness of effect.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 302, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 3#2"}, {"rank": 43, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 35", "name": "Making the vows", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Failing to make the vows — to maintain filial respect toward father, mother, teachers among the Saṃgha and the Three Jewels; to acquire good teachers, fellow students and good spiritual guides who will teach the Great Vehicle scriptures and the stages; to cultivate in accordance with the Dharma; and to uphold the Buddha's precepts solidly.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Aspiration must be formulated and stated rather than merely felt, and directed at the conditions that make practice possible — a good teacher, good companions, and the resolve to hold the precepts firmly.", "practical": "The offense clause penalises not making the vows. The text does not state a renewal interval; treating them as renewed weekly is a reader's judgement, not a textual finding.", "daily": "Reader's reading: renewed weekly rather than said once. In practice, a short weekly re-affirmation alongside whatever recitation the community keeps.", "term": "若一切菩薩不發是願者 ('if a bodhisattva does not make these vows')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Zhiyi, Zhuhong and Zhixu agree on the ten vows: filial respect toward parents and teachers among the Saṃgha; to acquire good teachers; to acquire fellow students and good spiritual friends; that they teach one the Great Vehicle sutras and moral codes; to understand the stages of advancement, the developmental stages, the vajra stages, and the grounds; to cultivate in accordance with the Dharma; and to uphold the Buddha's precepts solidly.", "emend": "", "canon": 13501, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 35#1"}, {"rank": 44, "tier": 2, "tier_label": "Daily practical", "precept": "Minor 36", "name": "Making the oaths", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Failing to swear the series of 'rather X than Y' oaths, and the vow that all beings attain buddhahood.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Sworn formulas of the 'rather X than Y' type, which oblige nothing described in the first half of each pair. Their working content is the second half: the precept-keeping to which each extremity is contrasted.", "practical": "Monastically framed — receiving donors' robes, food, seats — so several presuppose a mendicant life. The text states no renewal interval; weekly renewal is a reader's judgement.", "daily": "Reader's reading: renewed weekly. For a layholder several oaths describe a life not yet led; that is a framing mismatch, not a conflict.", "term": "寧…終不 ('rather… than')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13601, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 36#1"}, {"rank": 45, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 7", "name": "Attending Dharma teaching", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Failing to go and hear wherever scripture or vinaya is being expounded — forest, monastery room, house, anywhere at all.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Instruction must be actively sought wherever it is being given. The scope clause — forest, monastery room, house, anywhere at all — exists to remove the excuse of an inconvenient venue.", "practical": "The scope clause is the point: it removes the excuse of inconvenient venue.", "daily": "Attending talks and vinaya classes rather than skipping them for coursework.", "term": "若不至彼聽受者 ('if he does not go there to hear and receive')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 10701, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 7#1"}, {"rank": 46, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 6", "name": "Serving a visiting teacher", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "General orientation", "literal": "Failing to honour and serve an arriving Mahayana teacher — illustrated by rising to receive and see him off, bowing, supplying food, seating, medicine and all needs, requesting teaching three times daily, and doing so without anger or vexation arising. (The 'hundred flavors of food and drink worth three ounces of gold' daily is hyperbolic register, not a sum.)", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "A teacher's arrival creates an active obligation rather than an opportunity, and the duty to request teaching does real work: one is bound to ask, not merely to receive if offered. The exhortation is framed to make hospitality a form of practice rather than a courtesy.", "practical": "An orientation precept: the offense is a general failure of hospitality and reverence, not the omission of any single listed act.", "daily": "Being present when a visiting teacher arrives. Asking questions rather than attending passively. Offering to cover a real cost.", "term": "若不爾者 ('if not so') — the duty to request teaching is 常請法師三時說法 ('constantly request the teacher to expound three times daily')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The measure of gold is the old liang, about 1.1 ounces. Zhiyi clarifies that the three daily occasions are before noon, in the afternoon, and in the evening.", "emend": "", "canon": 10601, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 6#1"}, {"rank": 47, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 21", "name": "Retaliation", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Repaying anger with anger, or a blow with a blow — without compassion, deliberately.", "gate": "Without compassion / deliberate", "principle": "Vengeance is excluded absolutely, including on behalf of parents, kin and ruler, on the ground that repaying killing with killing does not accord with the way of reverence. The concluding clause names the renunciant specifically and requires the absence of compassion.", "practical": "Reciprocation as such, from verbal escalation upward.", "daily": "Replying in kind to a hostile email or comment. The most frequent form by far.", "term": "", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Fazang observes that although either a layperson or a monastic commits this offense, monastics are singled out to mark it as an especially deep fault in them.", "emend": "", "canon": 12101, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 21#1"}, {"rank": 48, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 21", "name": "Retaliation", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Taking revenge for the killing of parents, siblings or kin, or of the ruler — without compassion, deliberately.", "gate": "Without compassion / deliberate", "principle": "Vengeance is excluded absolutely, including on behalf of parents, kin and ruler, on the ground that repaying killing with killing does not accord with the way of reverence. The concluding clause names the renunciant specifically and requires the absence of compassion.", "practical": "Its modern form is supporting retributive action on behalf of a wronged group.", "daily": "Rare directly.", "term": "無慈報讎 ('without compassion avenging a wrong')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 12102, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 21#2"}, {"rank": 49, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 46", "name": "Teaching protocol", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "General orientation", "literal": "Expounding the Dharma otherwise than in accordance with the Dharma — illustrated by not standing to teach laypeople, sitting on a raised seat before the lay assembly, a bhiksu not standing on the ground to teach the four assemblies, the seat receiving incense and flowers, and listeners seated below.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "The Dharma is honoured through the physical arrangement of its delivery — who sits, who stands, who is raised and who is below. The concluding clause names a general failure to teach in accordance with the Dharma rather than any single breach of protocol.", "practical": "An orientation precept: the trigger is a general failure to teach in accordance with the Dharma, not the omission of any one listed arrangement. The posture element aligns broadly with the Theravada sekhiya rules; the high-seat-and-offerings protocol is a Chinese institutional form.", "daily": "Sitting rather than lecturing standing — including on video. Low friction unless one is the one teaching.", "term": "其說法者若不如法 ('if the one expounding does not do so in accordance with the Dharma')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 14601, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 46#1"}, {"rank": 50, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 20", "name": "Releasing and rescuing", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "General orientation", "literal": "Failing in the practice of releasing and rescuing — illustrated by constantly releasing living beings and teaching others to, intervening when an animal is being killed, teaching the bodhisattva precepts to save beings, and inviting a teacher to expound the precept scripture on the death-days of parents and siblings.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "The kinship of all beings across rebirths obliges action and not merely abstention — to kill and eat them is framed as killing and eating one's own parents. Release, intervention, teaching and the family death-day duty are all forms of the same obligation.", "practical": "Ceremonial release is now understood to cause ecological harm and to create a market for the animals released; the clause's concern is rescue.", "daily": "Rescue rather than purchase-and-release: intervening in cruelty, what one funds, what one eats. The death-day duty is a genuine one-off — worth knowing in advance whom to ask.", "term": "若不爾者 ('if not so') — the central duty is 常行放生 ('constantly practise release')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 12001, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 20#1"}, {"rank": 51, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 39", "name": "Cultivating merit and wisdom", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "General orientation", "literal": "A newly-trained bodhisattva failing to cultivate merit and wisdom — illustrated by establishing monastic dwellings, groves, fields, stupas and places for retreat and meditation, and by expounding the Mahayana texts at occasions of illness, danger, banditry, death-days and seven-week observances, travel and disaster, imprisonment, and for those heavy with lust, anger, delusion or disease.", "gate": "Newly-trained only", "principle": "Merit and wisdom are cultivated by maintaining the conditions under which a community can practise, and by owing textual knowledge outward at moments of crisis. Both halves are bounded by capacity.", "practical": "Reader's reading: infrastructure is listed first and is the item's primary work. Where the Mahayana texts are not the local liturgy, the community's own recitation serves — chanting at a funeral satisfies the recitation half as much as a talk does.", "daily": "Work periods, maintenance, building, and their digital equivalents — study tools and text archives count as durable establishment. Willingness to chant or read at a sickbed.", "term": "而新學菩薩若不爾者 ('if a newly-trained bodhisattva does not do so')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13901, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (Chinese)", ["The circumstances for cultivating merit and wisdom have come together.", "One is able to act.", "One fails to act."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "TWO EXPLICIT EXCEPTIONS, both important. First, capacity: 'You should cultivate according to your capacity. However much strength you have, you should do that much… The exception to the rule is if it's beyond your capacity. If you're talking, and nobody wants to listen to you, or you don't have any money at all to help build Sangha dwellings, then such activities are beyond your capacity.' Second, dedicated practice: 'if a person particularly cultivates a certain Dharma door, like Dhyana contemplation, and puts everything else down. Then if he doesn't go and build Sangha dwellings, that's excusable.'"}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "accords", "text": "Gives the same two exemptions and adds a third duty in their place: where one lacks the means to act, one should still rejoice in and praise the merit others are creating. A practitioner absorbed in meditation or recitation who has no leisure for other business does not offend, because he is already cultivating merit and wisdom by his own effort."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "On the recitation half: if one does not know how to lecture, reciting is enough — the Amitabha Sutra, the Earth Store Sutra, or the Brahma's Net itself. That removes the assumption that the duty requires competence to expound."}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 39#1"}, {"rank": 52, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 31", "name": "Ransoming", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Failing to take goods and ransom back sacred images, texts, or ordained persons being sold or forced into service.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "Means create obligation when the sacred or the vulnerable are being trafficked. The duty is active ransom, not sympathy, which is why it sits oddly on a mendicant and translates into advocacy and mobilising others.", "practical": "Requires resources, which is why it sits oddly on a mendicant, for whom the available form is advocacy and mobilising others.", "daily": "Looted Buddhist material at auction; trafficking and forced labour of religious minorities. Episodic.", "term": "若不贖者 ('if he does not ransom them')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13101, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 31#1"}, {"rank": 53, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 26", "name": "Receiving guest monks", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "As head of the Saṃgha dwelling, allowing the previously established Saṃgha members to accept a dānapati's invitation solely for themselves and failing to send the guest Saṃgha member in accordance with his position in the sequential order.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "The officer holds the rotation in trust; residents may not absorb what was offered to the whole community.", "practical": "A role-specific offense. It falls on whoever administers the invitation list, not on the monks who accepted in good faith.", "daily": "Dormant until you hold that office. Then it is about the roster, not about your own share.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "Restores 'wooden bed' to the list of requisites, and an entire clause absent from the Taisho: 'or should even be willing to cut off and sell his own flesh.'", "canon": 12601, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 26#1"}, {"rank": 54, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 28", "name": "Extending a separate invitation", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately extending a discriminatory invitation to a member of the Saṃgha, rather than entering the Saṃgha dwelling, inquiring of a steward, and inviting in accordance with the monastics' proper order.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Donors are directed to offer to the Sangha as a body rather than to chosen individuals, and are given the procedure for doing so. The comparison is stark: an invitation to five hundred arhats by name is worth less than one ordinary monk taken in turn.", "practical": "The Pali tradition ranks sangha-directed dana above individual-directed; the difference is that this makes the individual invitation an offense rather than merely lower merit.", "daily": "Binds lay donors directly. Route invitations through the monastery where a mechanism exists.", "term": "次第 ('in rotation')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: A worldly person's discriminatory invitation to five hundred arhats or bodhisattvas is worth less than an offering made in proper order to a single common-person Saṃgha member.", "emend": "Restores the steward's reply, so the passage reads as an exchange: the donor asks to invite the Saṃgha, and the steward answers with the merit comparison.", "canon": 12801, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 28#1"}, {"rank": 55, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 37", "name": "Reckless travel", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately entering dangerous places while on dhuta or in the rains residence — political danger, broken terrain, predators, hazards of water, fire and wind, bandits, snakes (grouped).", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Ascetic practice does not license unnecessary risk. The exhortation sets out the whole shape of the wandering life, but the penalty attaches only to deliberately entering danger.", "practical": "The calendar, the eighteen requisites and the fortnightly recitation carry no penalty — this clause is the whole of the offense.", "daily": "Some wandering-ascetic traditions deliberately seek predator country as part of the practice, which diverges from this clause.", "term": "難處 ('dangerous places')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The dhūta austerities number twelve, among them dwelling in a forest hermitage, always going on alms round in sequence without skipping houses, one meal a day, cast-off rag robes, dwelling in a charnel ground or beneath a tree, and never lying down to sleep. The commentators agree the two annual periods are spring and autumn. The tin-headed staff has rings that jingle to warn insects; the rope bed is most likely a hammock strung between trees.", "emend": "", "canon": 13701, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 17 不蓄三衣缽杖戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Failure to keep in reserve the three robes, a bowl and a ringed staff — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["A layperson is required to keep monastic requisites in reserve.", "The Brahma's Net lists eighteen requisites but attaches no penalty to them; here the omission is itself the offense."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 37#1"}, {"rank": 56, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 18", "name": "Teaching without understanding", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Acting as another's master and conferring precepts while not understanding a single line of them — having falsely claimed to understand.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "The falsity lies in the claim, not in the ignorance. Not knowing is no offense; claiming to know and conferring precepts on the strength of that claim is.", "practical": "Ignorance alone is not the offense; the pretense plus the conferral is.", "daily": "Taking on a teaching role slightly beyond what one knows and covering the gap.", "term": "詐言能解 ('falsely claiming to understand')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11801, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 24 僧不聽說法，輒自作戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Speaking Dharma without the Sangha's leave — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["The Brahma's Net bars teaching without understanding; this bars a layperson teaching without the saṅgha's permission at all, however well he understands."]}]}], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 18#1"}, {"rank": 57, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 12", "name": "Trafficking", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately trafficking in good people, male or female slaves, or the six kinds of domestic animals; trading in coffins, in the boards for making them, or in other products intended to contain the dead; or encouraging others to do so.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Commerce in persons, animals and the goods of death. The a fortiori construction makes procuring worse than doing it oneself, which inverts the usual grading and marks instruction as the graver form.", "practical": "The escalation 尚不自作況教人作 makes procuring worse than doing.", "daily": "Rare directly; its modern form is investment and supply-chain exposure.", "term": "尚不自作況教人作 ('not even oneself, still less instructing another')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11201, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 12#1"}, {"rank": 58, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Major 5", "name": "Selling alcohol", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Dealing in intoxicants oneself, or encouraging others to deal in them — thereby leading beings to develop minds beset by inverted views.", "gate": "Ungated", "principle": "The seller answers for the offenses his product enables, on the ground that intoxicants are the causal condition out of which other breaches arise. The precept governs commerce, not consumption — drinking falls under a light precept, while dealing is grave.", "practical": "A commercial act. Drinking falls under Minor 2 and is only a light offense.", "daily": "Employment, investment or fundraising in alcohol — and, on this reading, in cannabis and other recreational substances now legal in many jurisdictions.", "term": "是酒起罪因緣 ('alcohol is the causal condition from which offenses arise')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The same rendering choice as Minor 2: 'intoxicants' rather than 'alcoholic beverages', extended to any mind-altering substance consumed recreationally or habitually.", "emend": "", "canon": 501, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Major 6 酤酒戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Stricter", "bullets": [{"label": "Dealing in intoxicants — stricter here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Extends beyond alcohol to drugs and stimulants, matching Dharmamitra's rendering of the Brahma's Net term.", "It also states the 開緣 the Brahma's Net does not: a drink that looks and smells of alcohol but cannot intoxicate is no offense; selling medicinal wine that cannot derange is no offense; and alcohol may be taken where every other medicine has failed, or applied externally to a wound.", "But 受酒戒者不得抽菸、吸毒 (one who holds this precept may not smoke or take drugs)."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Intoxicants — 不飲酒 (no intoxicants). Alcohol, anaesthetics, tobacco, drugs and anything that costs a person his reason lead to shameless acts, waste money and time, and injure body and mind. Addiction harms oneself and others, and nothing damages family and society more."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "No offense where a drink looks and smells of alcohol but cannot intoxicate; where medicinal wine that cannot derange is sold; where alcohol is taken after every other medicine has failed; or where it is applied externally to a wound. Yeast and lees should not be eaten. One who holds this precept may not smoke or take drugs."}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 5#1"}, {"rank": 59, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 14", "name": "Arson", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Deliberately burning anything that has an owner — homes, towns, monastic quarters, fields, trees, property of spirits or officials; setting fires in forest and wild, especially in the fourth through ninth months.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Fire as indiscriminate destruction of habitat and property, with a seasonal restriction protecting the months of growth and nesting. The operative scope is ownership: nothing that has an owner may be burned.", "practical": "The owner clause is the operative scope; the seasonal restriction protects growth and nesting.", "daily": "Effectively dormant. Controlled burns and land clearance would be the only live form.", "term": "一切有主物 ('anything having an owner')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11401, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 14#1"}, {"rank": 60, "tier": 3, "tier_label": "Situational", "precept": "Minor 11", "name": "Military envoy", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Coming and going among troops, or acting as a traitor to one's country.", "gate": "Deliberate only", "principle": "Participation in the machinery of organised killing, whether by carrying commissions, raising forces, or simply moving among troops. The gain-and-malice gate governs the commissioning clause but not the clause about presence.", "practical": "Presence among armed forces, stated as a separate clause from carrying commissions.", "daily": "Military chaplaincy would sit here.", "term": "菩薩不得入軍中往來 ('a bodhisattva may not come and go among troops')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11101, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 11#1"}, {"rank": 61, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 30", "name": "Feigned intimacy and fast days", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Influencing laypeople such that they engage in killing beings, stealing, non-observance of the abstinence days, or otherwise breaking precepts on the six days of abstinence or during the year's three long months of abstinence.", "gate": "Malice", "principle": "Consistency between professed view and actual conduct, and restraint from drawing laypeople into breaking the observances they have undertaken. The two halves are graded differently: the go-between clause is a matter of restraint, while leading others to kill or steal offends against the nature.", "practical": "On Dharmamitra's text this is not about your own fast-day conduct at all. It concerns the effect of your influence on people who look to you.", "daily": "What you make permissible for others by example or by casual remark — especially people who treat your judgement as licence.", "term": "六齋日 ('the six fast days')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Zhixu titles this 'The Precept against Managing (or Exerting Influence on) the Laity', which is what fixes the subject as the laypeople rather than oneself. The six days of abstinence are the 8th, 14th, 15th, 23rd, 29th and 30th of the lunar month; the three long months are the 1st, 5th and 9th.", "emend": "Restores a line absent from the Taisho — 'if he serves as one who exerts influence on the laity' — present in nearly all commentarial quotations.", "canon": 13001, "conditions": ["Hui Seng (Chinese)", ["One has received the precepts (all five monastic assemblies; and the two lay assemblies once they have received the bodhisattva precepts).", "The day falls within the six fast days or three long fast months.", "One kills, steals, breaks the fast, or violates the precepts — or leads laypeople to do so."]], "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "accords", "text": "Independently supports the reading that the subject is influence over the laity: acting as a go-between is a violation of restraint, 'But if one influences laypeople to kill, to steal, or to break the Precepts of pure eating, that's encouraging them to actually violate Precepts' — and that, he says, is a violation against the nature, because it violates the major precepts against killing and stealing. So the two halves of the precept are graded differently."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (English)", "stance": "adds", "text": "Names the days: the 8th, 14th, 15th, 23rd, 29th and 30th of the lunar month, moving to the 28th and 29th in a short month; and the first, fifth and ninth lunar months. On the 8th and 23rd the messengers of the Four Heavenly Kings descend, on the 14th and 29th their royal sons, and on the 15th and 30th the Kings themselves — which is why an offense on those days is said to be graver than on other days."}, {"src": "Hui Seng (Chinese)", "stance": "adds", "text": "A lay precept-holder who fails to observe the six fast days and three fast months also offends — so the calendar binds householders directly and not only by way of the influence clause. States the precept is 惟遮不開, closed to exemption."}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "Standards of conduct — not offense clauses, but what the lay community holds", "items": ["Strictly uphold the six days of vegetarian fasting."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 30#1"}, {"rank": 62, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 9", "name": "Tending the sick", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Seeing a sick person — in monastery, town, wilderness or roadside — and not rescuing them.", "gate": "Malice / resentment", "principle": "Nursing the sick is named first among the eight fields of merit, and the sick person is to be attended as one would attend a buddha. The malice gate means that inability is never the offense; reluctance is.", "practical": "Gated on malice or resentment, so inability is not the offense. Reluctance is.", "daily": "Volunteering for sick-ward duty. Noticing someone unwell rather than assuming others have it.", "term": "看病福田第一 ('nursing the sick, first among the fields of merit')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 10901, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 3 不瞻病苦戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Laxer", "bullets": [{"label": "Failure to care for the sick — laxer here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["Supplies the exemption list the Brahma's Net lacks: no offense if one is ill oneself, lacks the strength, arranges for someone abler to attend, knows the person has family or can manage alone, if the illness is chronic or recurring, if one is engaged in a superior practice one does not wish to break off, or if one is already nursing another."]}]}, {"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook", "stance": "adds", "bullets": [{"label": "主旨 (the gist)", "text": "Compassion — 慈悲濟苦 (kindness and compassion relieving suffering). Kindness gives happiness; compassion removes suffering. 但願眾生得離苦，不為自身求安樂 (may beings be freed from suffering; I do not seek ease for myself) — may beings be freed from suffering; I do not seek ease for myself."}, {"label": "開緣及遮止 (exempting and restricting circumstances)", "text": "Sloth makes it an undefiled breach. No offense if one is ill oneself, lacks strength, arranges for someone abler, knows the person has family or can manage, if the illness is recurring or chronic, if one is engaged in a superior practice one does not wish to break off, if the person is hard to teach or hard to hold, or if one is already nursing another. The same applies to the destitute."}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 9#1"}, {"rank": 63, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Major 8", "name": "Stinginess", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Withholding even a coin, needle or blade of grass from a poor person who asks, or even a line or verse of Dharma from a seeker — and reviling him instead.", "gate": "Malice + anger", "principle": "Refusal combined with contempt, not poverty. Both material goods and the Dharma are treated as things one may not hoard, and the offense requires that the refusal be delivered with reviling — so having nothing to give is never the breach.", "practical": "Three elements together: malicious mind, refusal, and the reviling. Poverty is not a breach.", "daily": "Contempt for someone asking. Brushing off a beginner's question because it is elementary.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 801, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [{"src": "CTTB Upāsaka handbook · Minor 4 見乞不與戒", "stance": "diverges", "strictness": "Laxer", "bullets": [{"label": "Failure to be charitable to people in need — laxer here than in the Brahmā's Net", "items": ["A light precept where the Brahma's Net makes stinginess a grave one.", "Same exemption set as nursing the sick.", "The stated consequence is karmic rather than disciplinary: 慳財餘報，生生貧窮；慳法餘報，世世愚鈍 (stinting wealth brings poverty life after life; stinting Dharma brings dullness age after age)."]}]}], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Major 8#1"}, {"rank": 64, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 15", "name": "Perverse teaching", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Perversely teaching others the two-vehicle codes or non-Buddhist wrong-view treatises.", "gate": "Malice / anger", "principle": "Teaching that diverts a student from the vehicle toward which he was being led. Both the malicious mind and the perverse teaching are required, so explaining another tradition's texts for someone's benefit falls outside.", "practical": "Requires both the malicious mind and the perverse teaching. Explaining the suttas to someone they will help is not this.", "daily": "Dormant unless the malicious gate is actually open.", "term": "橫教 ('perversely teaching')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11501, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 15#1"}, {"rank": 65, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 13", "name": "Groundless accusation", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Alleging without cause that good people, teachers, monks, kings or nobles have committed the seven heinous acts or ten grave offenses — inflicting harm and bringing them to an unwanted state.", "gate": "Malice", "principle": "Reputational harm inflicted without cause on those who have standing to lose. Malice and groundlessness must join, which is what separates this from the positive duty to raise a well-founded charge within the community.", "practical": "Requires malice and groundlessness together. A well-founded report is Minor 5's duty, not this offense.", "daily": "Repeating an unverified allegation about a teacher. The gate is closed if one checks first.", "term": "", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The six close relatives are usually father, mother, elder and younger brothers, wife and children.", "emend": "The commentators read the filial and compassionate mind as owed to all beings, not only to the six close relatives; Dharmamitra follows them.", "canon": 11301, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 13#1"}, {"rank": 66, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 19", "name": "Sowing discord", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Seeing precept-holding bhikshus, censers in hand, practising the bodhisattva practices, and provoking hostility between two of them by slandering and deceiving such virtuous persons, saying of one to the other, 'There is no evil he will not do.'", "gate": "Malice", "principle": "Manufacturing conflict between practitioners, specifically by carrying a damaging account of one to the other. The malice gate excludes clumsy mediation and leaves deliberate provocation.", "practical": "On Dharmamitra's reading this is a specific slander uttered to set two practitioners against each other, not a general characterisation of a troublemaker.", "daily": "Carrying a version of events between two people that widens a rift.", "term": "鬪搆兩頭 ('constructing a quarrel between two sides')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: The Chinese term is literally 'double-tongued speech', which normally translates the Sanskrit paiśunya, usually understood as slander. Fazang's title: 'The Precept against Contentiousness-invoking Slander and Deception of Worthies.'", "emend": "", "canon": 11901, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 19#1"}, {"rank": 67, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 16", "name": "Withholding teaching", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Not answering what should be answered, expounding the texts jumbled and out of order, or speaking so as to disparage the Three Jewels.", "gate": "Gain", "principle": "The teaching must not be rationed, garbled or distorted for the teacher's advantage. The gate is the profit motive, and the offense is located in what is withheld or twisted rather than in any failure of skill.", "practical": "Gated on the profit motive. The burning of body, arm and fingers is exhortation and carries no penalty.", "daily": "Paywalling or withholding explanation. Garbling a text to make one's own reading look stronger.", "term": "為利養故 ('for the sake of gain')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11601, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 16#1"}, {"rank": 68, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 23", "name": "Answering questions", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Relying on one's understanding of the sutras and moral codes and on one's position as good spiritual friend of kings, princes and government officials, and then treating bodhisattvas new to the training with a slighting, offensive or arrogant attitude, failing to answer well every one of their questions.", "gate": "Slighting / malice / arrogance", "principle": "Access to explanation is owed to beginners, and proximity to power is no licence to ration it.", "practical": "Two components: the position relied upon, and the dismissive answer. The self-ordination and auspicious-sign material earlier in the item is setup.", "daily": "Answering a junior briskly because the question is basic — and doing so from a position of institutional standing.", "term": "輕心惡心慢心 ('a slighting, malicious, arrogant mind')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 12301, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 23#1"}, {"rank": 69, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 17", "name": "Soliciting on borrowed influence", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Cultivating rulers and officials and leaning on their power to demand, strike, drag or seize goods; excessive seeking, or inciting others to seek — wholly without compassion or reverence.", "gate": "Gain / fame", "principle": "Coercive fundraising conducted on borrowed power. Read in series with striking and dragging, the soliciting clause describes extortion rather than alms-seeking, and the concluding clause names the absence of compassion as part of the act.", "practical": "Read in the series with striking and dragging, this is shakedown, not pindapata.", "daily": "Using institutional or political connections to pressure donors. Repeated fundraising appeals.", "term": "恃作形勢 ('leaning on their power')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11701, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 17#1"}, {"rank": 70, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 29", "name": "Wrong livelihood", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Practising spells or artisan crafts for income.", "gate": "Malice + gain", "principle": "A mendicant lives on offerings, and any parallel income stream compromises the dependence that defines the life. The listed trades are alike in generating livelihood outside the alms economy, whatever their other merits.", "practical": "'Artisan crafts' is the clause that reaches makers and craftspeople.", "daily": "Selling handmade or fabricated objects. Worth noticing before a craft practice becomes a business.", "term": "工巧 ('artisan crafts')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 12901, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 29#1"}, {"rank": 71, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 29", "name": "Wrong livelihood", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Divination as a trade — physiognomy, dream-reading, fortune-telling, predicting a child's sex (grouped).", "gate": "Malice + gain", "principle": "A mendicant lives on offerings, and any parallel income stream compromises the dependence that defines the life. The listed trades are alike in generating livelihood outside the alms economy, whatever their other merits.", "practical": "Services a monk will be asked for, and the asking is often sincere.", "daily": "Astrological consultation, name-selection, auspicious dates. Monastics are asked for these often.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 12902, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 29#2"}, {"rank": 72, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 29", "name": "Wrong livelihood", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Producing food by one's own hand, grinding or pounding grain, as a source of income; trafficking in sexual services; falcon training; compounding poisons (grouped).", "gate": "Malice + gain", "principle": "A mendicant lives on offerings, and any parallel income stream compromises the dependence that defines the life. The listed trades are alike in generating livelihood outside the alms economy, whatever their other merits.", "practical": "Food production for income, not heating up one's own meal — parallel to the Vinaya's own cooking prohibition. The remainder is dormant in a modern setting.", "daily": "Selling food or running a kitchen business. Dormant as a layman, live once ordained.", "term": "自手作食、自磨自舂 ('cooking by one's own hand, grinding and pounding')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: 'Gold and silver poison' is variously explained: the name of a particular poison, a potion for driving away snakes in the fifth month, or the claim that gold and silver are themselves toxic enough to poison with. 'Gu poison' is a poison used in black magic to mobilise ghosts and spirits against others.", "emend": "", "canon": 12903, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 29#3"}, {"rank": 73, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 30", "name": "Feigned intimacy and fast days", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Slandering the Three Jewels by one's own actions even while deceptively appearing to be closely reliant on them; speaking in terms of emptiness even as one's actions betray involvement in existence; serving as a go-between for laymen and laywomen and thereby leading them into sensual attachment.", "gate": "Malice", "principle": "Consistency between professed view and actual conduct, and restraint from drawing laypeople into breaking the observances they have undertaken. The two halves are graded differently: the go-between clause is a matter of restraint, while leading others to kill or steal offends against the nature.", "practical": "Hypocrisy in a specific doctrinal register: using emptiness talk to license ordinary grasping.", "daily": "Invoking non-duality or skilful means to excuse something you would otherwise call a breach. Being drawn into lay relationships as a counsellor.", "term": "口便說空、行在有中 ('the mouth speaks emptiness, conduct is in existence')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13002, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 30#2"}, {"rank": 74, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 32", "name": "Harming beings", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Trading with light scales and short measures; seizing property by leaning on official power; binding people or wrecking what they have achieved.", "gate": "Harmful mind / deliberate", "principle": "Instruments and arrangements that structurally enable harm or exploitation — arms, false measures, coercive seizure, and the binding or wrecking of what others have built.", "practical": "Commercial dishonesty and abuse of position; the binding clause alone is gated on a harmful mind.", "daily": "Misrepresenting weights, quantities or figures in any accounting you handle.", "term": "害心 ('harmful mind')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 13202, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 32#2"}, {"rank": 75, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 33", "name": "Idle spectacle", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Watching fights between people, military engagements or brawls; acting as an agent for thieves.", "gate": "Malice", "principle": "Restraint of the mind's restless casting-about, which the traditional title names directly as initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. The five sub-restrictions are alike in fixing attention on what neither benefits the viewer nor anyone else.", "practical": "This clause alone is malice-gated, unlike the music and games clauses.", "daily": "Combat sport, war footage, fight videos. The gate closes if the interest is not appetitive.", "term": "以惡心故觀 ('with malicious mind, watching')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Zhiyi's title names 'wrong actions or thought', where 'thought' renders vitarka-vicāra — initial ideation and the discursion that follows it. Dharmamitra judges Fazang's title preferable here: 'The Precept against Watching, Listening, or Doing What Is Improper.'", "emend": "", "canon": 13307, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 33#7"}, {"rank": 76, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 11", "name": "Military envoy", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Carrying state commissions, assembling forces, or raising armies against one another.", "gate": "Gain / malice", "principle": "Participation in the machinery of organised killing, whether by carrying commissions, raising forces, or simply moving among troops. The gain-and-malice gate governs the commissioning clause but not the clause about presence.", "practical": "Gated on gain or malice; a chaplain serving individuals is not obviously in scope.", "daily": "Dormant for most, but bears directly on any military chaplaincy question.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 11102, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 11#2"}, {"rank": 77, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 42", "name": "Reciting before non-recipients", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Expounding these precepts before those who have not yet received the bodhisattva precepts, before evil non-Buddhists, or before those of wrong view — and to anyone else at all, excepting the kings of countries.", "gate": "Gain", "principle": "The text is restricted to those who have undertaken it, on the reasoning that exposure without commitment breeds contempt. The profit motive gates the clause, which narrows it considerably.", "practical": "Gated on the profit motive, which narrows it considerably. The text is publicly available anyway, so secrecy is not achievable.", "daily": "Not making the precepts into public content. Self-resolving otherwise, since solo recitation is provided for.", "term": "為利養故 ('for the sake of gain')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 14201, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 42#1"}, {"rank": 78, "tier": 4, "tier_label": "Gated by ill intent (malice / gain / harm)", "precept": "Minor 48", "name": "Destroying the Dharma from within", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "For the sake of fame or personal benefit, speaking the Buddha's precepts in the presence of a king or any of the hundred officials in such a way as to cause bhikshus, bhikshunis or bodhisattva-precept disciples to become subject to imprisonment, or to rules like those appropriate for prisoners, soldiers or servants.", "gate": "Fame / gain", "principle": "Damage inflicted from inside that no outsider could accomplish — the parasite in the lion's body. The fault is using the precepts themselves as a lever against fellow practitioners before external authority.", "practical": "Using the precepts as a lever against fellow practitioners before external authority.", "daily": "Invoking rules to constrain colleagues in front of an institution or governing body.", "term": "如師子身中蟲 ('like worms in the lion's body')", "note": "", "emend": "Drops 'seven' as a relatively modern interpolation absent from the early commentaries and all four other editions; restores 'precept' to 'bodhisattva-precept disciples', the prisoner-and-soldier clause, the parasite-in-the-lion passage, and 'One cannot damage them'.", "canon": 14801, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Anyone holding the precepts", "key": "Minor 48#1"}, {"rank": 79, "tier": 5, "tier_label": "Dormant or one-off", "precept": "Minor 25", "name": "Monastic office", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Aggravating disputes that arise within the Saṃgha, or using the property of the Three Jewels in a careless manner, as though it were one's own.", "gate": "Office-holder only", "principle": "Custodial property is held in trust and not at the officer's discretion. The two named faults — spending without limit as though it were one's own, and throwing the assembly into strife — are the two ways an office can be misused.", "practical": "Triggers only when you hold one of the named offices — teaching, monastery, instruction, meditation, travel.", "daily": "Dormant until you hold responsibility. Then continuous.", "term": "恣心用三寶物 ('using the Three Jewels' property at whim')", "note": "", "emend": "", "canon": 12501, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 25#1"}, {"rank": 80, "tier": 5, "tier_label": "Dormant or one-off", "precept": "Minor 40", "name": "Non-discrimination in conferral", "trigger": "Omission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Failing to confer the precepts promptly on one who has come a hundred or a thousand li seeking them.", "gate": "Teacher role + malice or hatred", "principle": "Radical non-selection in conferring the precepts, with an enumerated list of eligible recipients that deliberately includes the socially despised and the non-human. The single hard exclusion is the one who has committed a heinous act in this life.", "practical": "An honest judgment of readiness is not the offense; contempt dressed as discernment is. The bar on conferring upon one who has committed the seven heinous acts, and the instruction that the kasaya be 壞色 ('spoiled in colour'), are 不得/應教 statements carrying no 犯 clause of their own — real prohibitions, but not offense triggers.", "daily": "Who gets access to teaching and community. Filtering by background, education, respectability or status.", "term": "不得蕑擇 ('no selection')", "note": "", "emend": "Restores 'an attitude affected by hatred' alongside the bad attitude, from the Song, Yuan, Ming and Gong editions and all the Tiantai commentaries.", "canon": 14001, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 40#1"}, {"rank": 81, "tier": 5, "tier_label": "Dormant or one-off", "precept": "Minor 41", "name": "Conferring precepts for gain", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Conferring the precepts while falsely appearing to understand the texts, having sought profitable disciples.", "gate": "Teacher role + gain", "principle": "The teaching role must not become a means of accumulation, nor be occupied by someone who has to feign competence to hold it. The conferral is the act; the greed and the pretense are what make it an offense.", "practical": "The pretense element is what would catch a serious person, not the greed. On the remedial system the item sets out: a breach of the ten requires repentance before images until an auspicious sign appears; a breach of the forty-eight is extinguished by repenting before a superior member of the Saṃgha — Dharmamitra reads the term as requiring someone senior, not merely another person.", "daily": "The economy of Dharma teaching — paid retreats, platforms, book sales. And covering a gap in what you know.", "term": "詐現解一切經律 ('falsely appearing to understand all the scriptures and codes')", "note": "Dharmamitra footnotes: Zhixu holds that in this transmission Śākyamuni is the upādhyāya and Maitreya the ācārya, and would repunctuate the Chinese accordingly; Dharmamitra declines to follow him, since neither Zhiyi nor Fazang supports it.", "emend": "", "canon": 14101, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Monastics and preceptors", "key": "Minor 41#1"}, {"rank": 82, "tier": 5, "tier_label": "Dormant or one-off", "precept": "Minor 47", "name": "Legislating against the Dharma", "trigger": "Commission", "form": "Specific act", "literal": "Enacting laws restricting the four assemblies; forbidding going forth; forbidding images, stupas and texts; appointing controllers and registering monks; making monastics stand while laypeople sit high (grouped).", "gate": "Ruler / official only", "principle": "Precept-holders who hold political power may not use it to constrain the Sangha. The enumerated measures are administrative rather than violent, which is what makes the precept unusually specific.", "practical": "Addressed to rulers and officials who have themselves received the precepts.", "daily": "Dormant. Its nearest form would be institutional policy restricting practice.", "term": "", "note": "", "emend": "A sixty-one character passage is set in braces as an emendation — the registration of monks, bhikshus made to stand while laypeople sit on high seats, and monastics reduced to subservient messengers obeying officials.", "canon": 14701, "conditions": null, "fourfold": null, "voices": [], "scope": "Rulers and officials", "key": "Minor 47#1"}], "upasaka": [{"id": "Major 1", "en": "Killing", "zh": "殺戒", "maps": "Major 1", "stance": "Same", "note": "Both are the gravest offense in their set. The handbook's 開緣 are narrow: death caused without intent to kill, and severe derangement — 見火而捉，如金無異 (seizing fire as though it were gold) ('seizing fire as though it were gold'), which it distinguishes from mere agitation.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Major 2", "en": "Stealing", "zh": "盜戒", "maps": "Major 2", "stance": "Same", "note": "不與而取謂之盜 (taking what is not given is theft). Its 開緣 are borrowed from the Yogacara precepts: a bodhisattva may seize goods back from robbers out of compassion, but must return every item to its owner — 倘分毫沾染，是名為賊 'if he keeps so much as a fraction, he is the thief.'", "unique": false}, {"id": "Major 3", "en": "Major false speech", "zh": "大妄語戒", "maps": "Major 4", "stance": "Laxer", "note": "Narrower than the Brahma's Net, which makes all deceit the grave offense. Here only the great false speech — claiming attainment — is a major precept; ordinary lying, frivolous speech, double-tongued speech and harsh speech fall to the light class. The 開緣 are broad: lying to save beings from danger to life, and lying to benefit others where one has no attachment of one's own.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Major 4", "en": "Lustful behavior", "zh": "邪婬 (sexual misconduct)戒", "maps": "Major 3", "stance": "Same", "note": "Both narrow to misconduct for a householder. The handbook's 開緣 cover deep sleep, derangement, and being seized by an enemy where there is 惟苦無樂 (suffering only, no pleasure), suffering and no pleasure.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Major 5", "en": "Speaking of offenses of the fourfold assembly", "zh": "說四眾過戒", "maps": "Major 6", "stance": "Laxer", "note": "Names the fourfold assembly where the Brahma's Net commentary extends to the whole sevenfold assembly. Otherwise identical in shape, and a major precept in both.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Major 6", "en": "Dealing in intoxicants", "zh": "酤酒戒", "maps": "Major 5", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "Extends beyond alcohol to drugs and stimulants, matching Dharmamitra's rendering of the Brahma's Net term. It also states the 開緣 the Brahma's Net does not: a drink that looks and smells of alcohol but cannot intoxicate is no offense; selling medicinal wine that cannot derange is no offense; and alcohol may be taken where every other medicine has failed, or applied externally to a wound. But 受酒戒者不得抽菸、吸毒 (one who holds this precept may not smoke or take drugs).", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 1", "en": "Failure to make offerings to parents, teachers and elders", "zh": "不供養父母師長戒", "maps": "Minor 1", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "The Brahma's Net names teachers and seniors; this adds parents, and makes the omission itself the offense. Its group is 惟遮不開 (prescribed, and closed to exemption).", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 2", "en": "Indulging in intoxicants for pleasure", "zh": "耽樂飲酒戒", "maps": "Minor 2", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "Covers alcohol, anaesthetics, tobacco, drugs and 任何能使人喪失理智之藥品 (anything that costs a person his reason). The reasoning is social as much as personal: 一旦上癮更是害人害己；破壞家庭及社會 (once addicted one harms others and oneself, and wrecks family and society).", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 3", "en": "Failure to care for the sick", "zh": "不瞻病苦戒", "maps": "Minor 9", "stance": "Laxer", "note": "Supplies the exemption list the Brahma's Net lacks: no offense if one is ill oneself, lacks the strength, arranges for someone abler to attend, knows the person has family or can manage alone, if the illness is chronic or recurring, if one is engaged in a superior practice one does not wish to break off, or if one is already nursing another.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 4", "en": "Failure to be charitable to people in need", "zh": "見乞不與戒", "maps": "Major 8", "stance": "Laxer", "note": "A light precept where the Brahma's Net makes stinginess a grave one. Same exemption set as nursing the sick. The stated consequence is karmic rather than disciplinary: 慳財餘報，生生貧窮；慳法餘報，世世愚鈍 (stinting wealth brings poverty life after life; stinting Dharma brings dullness age after age).", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 5", "en": "Failure to be hospitable to and bow to elders", "zh": "見四眾尊長不承禮拜戒", "maps": "Minor 1", "stance": "Same", "note": "Its 開緣 are unusually practical and apply to the Brahma's Net clause too: no offense if gravely ill or of disordered mind; none while a senior is speaking Dharma or a serious decision is being deliberated; none while oneself is speaking or listening to Dharma; and none 若護僧制 (to uphold the saṅgha)s regulations' — where the saṅgha's own regulations require otherwise.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 6", "en": "Arrogance on seeing the fourfold assembly violate precepts", "zh": "見四眾毀戒心生憍慢戒", "maps": "Minor 5", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "Where the Brahma's Net requires raising a transgressor's offense, this adds that one may not feel superior on witnessing it. The disposition is policed, not only the omission.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 7", "en": "Failure to observe the six days of vegetarian fasting", "zh": "不持六齋戒", "maps": "Minor 30", "stance": "Same", "note": "Confirms that the fast calendar binds lay precept-holders directly, which is what the Chinese of the Brahma's Net clause also says and its English drops.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 8", "en": "Failure to go to listen to the Dharma", "zh": "不往聽法戒", "maps": "Minor 7", "stance": "Same", "note": "Same duty, same shape.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 9", "en": "Taking items belonging to the Sangha", "zh": "受用僧物戒", "maps": "Minor 27", "stance": "Same", "note": "The lay counterpart of the separate-invitation problem: what belongs to the saṅgha of the ten directions is not to be taken into private use.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 10", "en": "Drinking water that contains bugs", "zh": "飲蟲水戒", "maps": "Major 1", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "Grouped under killing. The Brahma's Net has nothing this fine-grained; here the standard reaches to what one drinks without looking.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 11", "en": "Travelling alone through dangerous places", "zh": "險難獨行戒", "maps": "Minor 37", "stance": "Same", "note": "The same concern as the Brahma's Net dhūta clause, but binding a layperson and without the ascetic framing — which removes the tension between the clause and traditions that seek out wild country deliberately.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 12", "en": "Staying overnight unchaperoned in a convent or monastery", "zh": "獨宿尼寺戒", "maps": "", "stance": "Unique", "note": "No Brahma's Net counterpart. A propriety rule protecting both parties from the appearance of impropriety.", "unique": true}, {"id": "Minor 13", "en": "Beating people as a livelihood or for a wage", "zh": "為財打人戒", "maps": "Minor 32", "stance": "Same", "note": "Grouped under killing and harming rather than under livelihood, which is where the Brahma's Net would place it.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 14", "en": "Bringing leftover food to offer to the fourfold assembly", "zh": "殘食施四眾戒", "maps": "", "stance": "Unique", "note": "No Brahma's Net counterpart. Grouped with offerings to parents, teachers and the Three Jewels, and 惟遮不開 (prescribed and closed to exemption) — closed to exemption.", "unique": true}, {"id": "Minor 15", "en": "Keeping cats and other predatory animals", "zh": "蓄貓狸戒", "maps": "Minor 32", "stance": "Same", "note": "The Brahma's Net names cats, raccoon dogs, pigs and dogs; this names predators specifically, and places it under killing rather than under harm.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 16", "en": "Raising animals without performing pure giving", "zh": "蓄養畜獸不淨施 (pure giving)戒", "maps": "Minor 32", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "Adds a requirement the Brahma's Net does not have: an animal kept must be formally given away (淨施 (pure giving)) rather than owned outright.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 17", "en": "Failure to keep in reserve the three robes, a bowl and a ringed staff", "zh": "不蓄三衣缽杖戒", "maps": "Minor 37", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "A layperson is required to keep monastic requisites in reserve. The Brahma's Net lists eighteen requisites but attaches no penalty to them; here the omission is itself the offense.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 18", "en": "Failure to look for pure water and land for crops", "zh": "作田不求淨水陸種處戒", "maps": "", "stance": "Unique", "note": "No Brahma's Net counterpart. Grouped under killing: farming must not be done where it destroys life in the water or soil.", "unique": true}, {"id": "Minor 19", "en": "Trading with altered weights and measures", "zh": "市易販賣斗秤不平戒", "maps": "Minor 32", "stance": "Same", "note": "The same clause the Brahma's Net has, as a standalone precept here.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 20", "en": "Conjugal relations at the wrong times or places", "zh": "非時非處行欲戒", "maps": "Major 3", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "The Brahma's Net leaves the lay scope at 邪婬 (sexual misconduct) without specifying. This adds time and place restrictions for married couples — 若非時非處行欲，則傷身敗性，違背禮儀 (relations at the wrong time or place harm the body, mar the nature, and go against propriety).", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 21", "en": "Failure to pay official taxes when doing business", "zh": "商賈不輸官稅戒", "maps": "", "stance": "Unique", "note": "No Brahma's Net counterpart. Grouped under stealing: unpaid tax is treated as taking what is not given.", "unique": true}, {"id": "Minor 22", "en": "Breaking federal and civil laws", "zh": "犯國制戒", "maps": "", "stance": "Unique", "note": "No Brahma's Net counterpart, and not to be confused with its Minor 47, which binds rulers who legislate against the Dharma. This binds the ordinary subject to ordinary law.", "unique": true}, {"id": "Minor 23", "en": "Failure to offer new food to the Triple Jewel first", "zh": "得新食，不先供三寶戒", "maps": "", "stance": "Unique", "note": "No Brahma's Net counterpart. Closed to exemption, with its group's whole point being that 三寶是無上福田 (the Three Jewels are the unsurpassed field of merit).", "unique": true}, {"id": "Minor 24", "en": "Speaking Dharma without the Sangha's leave", "zh": "僧不聽說法，輒自作戒", "maps": "Minor 18", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "The Brahma's Net bars teaching without understanding; this bars a layperson teaching without the saṅgha's permission at all, however well he understands.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 25", "en": "Walking ahead of the fivefold assembly", "zh": "在五眾前行戒", "maps": "Minor 38", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "Extends the seniority principle from where one sits to where one walks.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 26", "en": "Apportioning food to the Sangha unfairly", "zh": "僧食不公分戒", "maps": "Minor 26", "stance": "Same", "note": "The lay side of the same distribution duty the Brahma's Net lays on the officer.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 27", "en": "Raising silkworms", "zh": "養蠶戒", "maps": "Major 1", "stance": "Stricter", "note": "Grouped under killing. A specific livelihood the Brahma's Net does not name.", "unique": false}, {"id": "Minor 28", "en": "Abandoning sick people encountered while travelling", "zh": "行路見病捨去戒", "maps": "Minor 9", "stance": "Same", "note": "The triggered form of the duty to nurse: an encounter on the road, not a standing obligation.", "unique": false}], "legend": [["Source of truth", "Bhikshu Dharmamitra's Kalavinka translation (annotated). His Chinese is an emended text, collated against the Song, Yuan, Ming and Gong editions and against the sutra as quoted inside the Zhiyi, Zhuhong and Zhixu commentaries — so in places it is not the same text the Taisho preserves."], ["Emended by Dharmamitra", "Marks a clause where Dharmamitra's Chinese departs from the Taisho — a restored line, word or phrase. Fourteen clauses carry one."], ["Who it binds", "Whether a clause binds anyone holding the precepts, only monastics and preceptors, or only rulers and officials. A filter isolates the restricted ones."], ["Sources", "Every commentarial statement names its source. Nothing appears without one."], ["Commentarial note", "Substantive glosses from Zhiyi, Fazang, Zhuhong and Zhixu, and Dharmamitra's own interpretive judgements. Purely editorial and bibliographic apparatus is not reproduced."], ["Commentary layer", "A third layer, kept separate from the translations so sources can be compared rather than merged. Each voice is marked ACCORDS (independently reaches the same reading), ADDS (supplies what the other sources lack), or DIVERGES (genuinely conflicts — both sides are shown and neither is merged away). Currently populated for Major 6 and Major 7 from Hui Seng's commentary."], ["Conditions", "Where a commentary enumerates the conditions that must ALL be met for the offense to be complete, they are listed in order. This is the commentary answering the same question the chart asks."], ["Causes / conditions / methods / karma", "The fourfold analysis the sutra names in every major precept but never explains. Supplied from commentary where available."], ["Precept titles", "Dharmamitra states that the scripture itself contains no titles for any precept. He uses Zhiyi's titles and gives Fazang's in his notes; all printed headings are editorial."], ["Taisho baseline", "T24n1484, read from the CBETA P5 XML, remains the baseline against which every Chinese term here is checked."], ["What is here", "ONLY clauses that trigger an offense. Aspirational and exhortative material carrying no penalty is excluded — burning fingers, selling one's children, three taels of gold as a sum, rejoicing at a killing, the auspicious-sign procedure, the dhuta calendar, the eighteen requisites, the fortnightly recitation."], ["Locating the trigger", "The pivot 而 ('but instead') separates the ideal from the charge; the offense runs from there to 者 ('the one who'). Absent a pivot, the formula is 若故…者 ('if deliberately…then') or 若不爾者 ('if not so'). Only material inside that span appears."], ["Completing conditions", "Merged into each clause they complete rather than listed apart. Major 4's 'producing wrong speech, view or action in beings' therefore appears on all six of its clauses, because it completes each of them."], ["Clause form: Specific act", "The offense clause names a discrete act or omission."], ["Clause form: General orientation", "The offense clause names a failure of overall disposition or activity, with the body of the precept giving hyperbolic illustration. Minor 45's trigger is 若不教化眾生者 ('if he does not teach and transform beings') — not the calling out to people or the addressing of animals, which illustrate the orientation. These precepts close off the solitary practitioner who withdraws from beings. Minor 1, 6, 20, 39, 44 and 45 take this form."], ["Trigger: Commission", "The offense is doing the thing named."], ["Trigger: Omission", "The offense is failing to do the thing named, from clauses reading 若不爾者 ('if not so')."], ["Gate", "The mental state or motive the clause requires. 'Ungated' means the act alone suffices. Gain-, malice- and harm-gated clauses sit in Tier 4."], ["Literal vs practical", "'Offense clause (literal)' is what the text says; 'Practical reading' is how it plausibly applies, including where a literal reading is impossible or counterproductive. Divergences are flagged rather than smoothed."], ["Sort order", "Two orders are available in the dashboard: by intrusiveness (the default, ranked 1 to 82) and by canonical order (Major 1–10, then Minor 1–48)."], ["Tiers 1–3", "Ordered by how often the clause touches ordinary life, continuous to event-triggered. All ungated."], ["Tier 4", "Requires malice, profit motive or intent to harm. Ranked low because that gate is already closed by how you operate — not because the clause is minor."], ["Band 5 vs the scope filter", "Two different things that used to share a name. The band 'Dormant or one-off' is about FREQUENCY — the clause lies idle until you hold an office, or is discharged by saying it once. The filter 'Monastics / rulers only' is about WHO IS LIABLE — the offense clause makes only monastics, preceptors or rulers answerable. A clause can be in one without the other."], ["Penalty", "Major 1–10: parajika. Minor 1–48: light defiling offense, cleared by face-to-face confession (對首懺)."], ["Caveat", "Clause splits, gates, practical readings, daily implications and ranking are editorial judgements, not the text's own categories. Commentarial material reaches this chart only through Dharmamitra's notes; the commentaries themselves have not been read directly."]]};
